WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.930
Morning, Ziad.

00:00:00.930 --> 00:00:02.723
Tuesday the twenty-seventh.

00:00:02.723 --> 00:00:08.702
Calendar's got a long one, starting with a run and ending with a class around nine-thirty.

00:00:08.702 --> 00:00:11.159
Here's one to chew on while you move.

00:00:11.159 --> 00:00:16.009
When a podcast app checks the feed, how does it decide an episode is new?

00:00:16.009 --> 00:00:17.337
Answer's at the end.

00:00:17.337 --> 00:00:18.333
Weather's easy.

00:00:18.333 --> 00:00:22.850
Mostly sunny, around sixty, barely any wind, and dry into the night.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:23.780
Okay, the day.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:27.699
Calendar has an easy zone two run at seven, thirty minutes.

00:00:27.699 --> 00:00:29.559
Then Godinger, nine to five.

00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:37.995
There's an appointment on there at four-thirty, which sits right inside the workday, so that one might need a little shuffling.

00:00:37.995 --> 00:00:40.520
Another appointment's on there at six.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:46.033
And the calendar closes with a slow class at eight-thirty, wrapping at nine-thirty.

00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:51.214
Between six and eight-thirty there's a real gap, so that's your window to eat.

00:00:51.214 --> 00:00:52.144
Alright, news.

00:00:52.144 --> 00:00:57.923
Just Eat, the British food delivery app, tried filling in missing menu photos with A.I.

00:00:57.923 --> 00:00:58.853
It went badly.

00:00:58.853 --> 00:01:01.709
The model read the menus way too literally.

00:01:01.709 --> 00:01:05.828
A jacket potato came out as a winter coat covered in potatoes.

00:01:05.828 --> 00:01:09.747
A tiger prawn dish had actual tiger cubs swimming in sauce.

00:01:09.747 --> 00:01:15.459
And a bacon and egg soda bread showed up as a glass of cola with bacon floating in it.

00:01:15.459 --> 00:01:21.438
The company apologized and paused the rollout, but it says it's still working on the tool.

00:01:21.438 --> 00:01:29.077
For anyone who shoots product for a living, it's a decent reminder of what a person with a camera actually catches.

00:01:29.077 --> 00:01:38.044
OpenAI says its models produced new results in math, computer science, and physics, including work on a couple of famous open problems.

00:01:38.044 --> 00:01:42.694
That's a big claim, and it hasn't been checked by outside experts yet.

00:01:42.694 --> 00:01:46.945
So treat it as a maybe until other mathematicians go through it.

00:01:46.945 --> 00:01:48.738
And Israel is voting today.

00:01:48.738 --> 00:01:53.654
Turnout this morning was running at its highest since nineteen eighty-one.

00:01:53.654 --> 00:02:03.950
Polls close around four this afternoon our time, and exit polls come right after, so by tonight you'll know whether it's the stalemate the polls predicted.

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:06.474
Here's one for the weekend after next.

00:02:06.474 --> 00:02:13.183
On Saturday the seventh, Elsewhere has a jazz night with Kamasi Washington and the singer Ami Taf Ra.

00:02:13.183 --> 00:02:16.172
It's twenty-five dollars and starts at seven.

00:02:16.172 --> 00:02:24.342
Kamasi's the big name, but it's a good excuse to hear someone you probably don't know yet, and it's a short ride from home.

00:02:24.342 --> 00:02:26.667
Today's challenge is for your body.

00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:32.114
On the run this morning, breathe only through your nose for the first ten minutes.

00:02:32.114 --> 00:02:38.358
If you can't keep it up, that's your signal you're going too fast, so slow down until you can.

00:02:38.358 --> 00:02:42.543
It's a built-in speed limit for an easy day, no watch required.

00:02:42.543 --> 00:02:45.266
Okay, something new today, from lighting.

00:02:45.266 --> 00:02:51.178
It's called the inverse square law, and it explains a lot of what you already do by feel.

00:02:51.178 --> 00:02:53.304
Light spreads out as it travels.

00:02:53.304 --> 00:02:59.747
So when you double the distance between a light and your subject, the light doesn't drop by half.

00:02:59.747 --> 00:03:01.209
It drops to a quarter.

00:03:01.209 --> 00:03:03.932
Triple the distance, and you get a ninth.

00:03:03.932 --> 00:03:09.711
That's why moving a light just a little can change your exposure a lot when it's close.

00:03:09.711 --> 00:03:13.497
Going from one foot to two feet costs you two full stops.

00:03:13.497 --> 00:03:16.885
But going from ten feet to eleven barely registers.

00:03:16.885 --> 00:03:18.413
Here's the useful part.

00:03:18.413 --> 00:03:21.136
Falloff matters too, not just brightness.

00:03:21.136 --> 00:03:30.569
With a light very close, the front of a product can be bright while the back goes dark fast, because the back is relatively much farther away.

00:03:30.569 --> 00:03:37.145
Pull the light way back, turn the power up, and the whole object lights more evenly, front to back.

00:03:37.145 --> 00:03:39.868
So it's a choice you can make on purpose.

00:03:39.868 --> 00:03:43.588
Close light gives you drama and a fast fade into shadow.

00:03:43.588 --> 00:03:45.514
Far light gives you evenness.

00:03:45.514 --> 00:03:46.179
One catch.

00:03:46.179 --> 00:03:51.360
A light that's closer also looks bigger to the subject, so it gets softer too.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.279
Pulling it back buys evenness but makes it a little harder.

00:03:55.279 --> 00:04:03.914
Next time a piece of silver looks bright on one end and muddy on the other, try backing the light off before you add a second one.

00:04:03.914 --> 00:04:11.287
So: calendar says run at seven, Godinger till five, appointments at four-thirty and six, class at eight-thirty.

00:04:11.287 --> 00:04:15.472
And the answer: it checks the episode's G.U.I.D., a unique I.D.

00:04:15.472 --> 00:04:17.000
Have an easy run, Ziad.
